This project implements a Binary Search Tree (BST) using C++. It allows users to enter node values, perform inorder traversal, display leaf nodes, count the total number of nodes, and calculate the height of each node. It helps understand tree data structures and their basic operations.

Modified problem statement
The project aims to construct a Binary Search Tree (BST) using a user-defined insertion sequence and perform tree traversals. It also identifies leaf nodes, counts the total number of nodes, and calculates the height of each node to demonstrate important BST operations.

Algorithm

Step 1: Start.

Step 2: Initialize the BST root to NULL.

Step 3: Accept the number of nodes from the user.

Step 4: Read the node values and insert each value into the BST according to BST rules.

Step 5: Perform inorder traversal and display the nodes in ascending order.

Step 6: Traverse the BST and display all leaf nodes.

Step 7: Count all nodes and display the total number of nodes.

Step 8: Calculate and display the height of each node.

Step 9: Calculate and display the overall height of the BST.

Step 10: Release allocated memory.

Step 11: Stop.


